{"title":"LeafCart","sub":"Every grocery order earns 86 cents","type":"Profitability","industry":"Online marketplace","geo":"Canada","diff":2,"fmt":"Interviewer-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Unit economics · marketplace levers · break-even","prompt":"LeafCart delivers groceries from local supermarkets in Toronto in under an hour. It handles 1.2 million orders a year but loses money overall. Investors ask whether it can ever be profitable, and what it would take.","clarify":[{"q":"How does LeafCart earn money?","a":"18% commission from the supermarket on each basket, plus a $3.99 delivery fee from the customer."},{"q":"Average basket?","a":"$45."},{"q":"Costs per order?","a":"Courier pay $7.50, card fees 2.5% of the basket, customer support $0.60, and promotions (discount codes) averaging $2.00."},{"q":"Fixed costs?","a":"About $6M a year for technology, marketing staff and the office."}],"structure":{"root":"How can LeafCart become profitable?","branches":[{"head":"Revenue per order","items":["Basket size","Commission","Delivery fee"]},{"head":"Cost per order","items":["Courier","Card fees","Support","Promotions"]},{"head":"Volume vs fixed costs","items":["Orders needed to break even"]}]},"hypothesis":"Per order, the margin is tiny, so more volume alone won't fix this. Courier cost is the biggest item, so batching orders and bigger baskets are likely the main levers.","questions":[{"q":"What does LeafCart earn on an average order?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>Revenue: 18% × $45 = $8.10 + $3.99 fee = $12.09. Costs: $7.50 + $1.13 + $0.60 + $2.00 = $11.23. Contribution: <b>$0.86 an order</b>.</p> <p>On 1.2M orders that is $1.0M, far below $6M of fixed costs. Break-even would need 7.0M orders, about 5.8× today.</p>"},{"q":"Management has three ideas. Size each.","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"372.76pt\" height=\"215.96pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 372.76 215.96\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><d